{"amendments":[{"body":"crates/tank_core/tests/reproduction_factors.rs:888-932 only records `max_tan`, `max_no2`, and `max_instability` from one snapshot per `run_day()` call, after 24 simulated hours have already elapsed. That means the new 'stable tank keeps chemistry in range' assertions cannot see any intra-day TAN/NO2/instability spikes between the feed at hour 0 and the day-end snapshot. A regression that creates dangerous hourly excursions but settles by the end of the day would still pass this test.","rationale":"This is a false-negative in the new integration coverage: the test now claims continuous chemistry stability, but it only samples day-end state."},{"body":"crates/tank_core/tests/reproduction_factors.rs:243-289 no longer isolates instability as the reason readiness stays low after a swing. The test now drives the tank to 36 C and then checks for 2-3 days of reduced readiness, but that same pattern can be produced by the direct temperature penalty and the condition/molt damage from the hot day even if post-swing readiness stops consulting `stability_tracker.instability_index`. The test verifies that instability remains elevated, but it does not prove that lingering instability is what is suppressing readiness.","rationale":"This weakens the regression guard for the actual post-swing instability mechanic the test is meant to cover."}],"summary":"Reviewed the working-tree diff and read the changed implementation/test files. The only production-code change in `crates/tank_core/src/systems/shrimp.rs` is formatting; the actionable findings are both test-coverage regressions in the rewritten `crates/tank_core/tests/reproduction_factors.rs` scenarios."}